From 7e526f03588aa5478de47a00adff01ab4bc4967a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sccache patch <patch@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:21:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] util: reuse file digests when metadata is unchanged

Some inputs are hashed for every compilation that mentions them: the .ifc
files found through /ifcSearchDir and /stdIfcDir are an example, and a
standard library BMI or IFC easily reaches hundreds of megabytes. Reading
them back from disk on every compile is pure overhead.

Keep the digest of a file in a process-wide cache keyed by its path, size,
mtime and ctime, and reuse it while those still match. Two restrictions keep
this safe:

* Only files that had already been left untouched for HASH_CACHE_MIN_AGE are
  cached, so a file that is still being written within the timestamp
  resolution of the file system is always read again.
* Only absolute paths are cached, since a relative path would be resolved
  against the working directory of the server rather than of the
  compilation.

The cache is bounded and only saves work, so it is dropped wholesale when it
grows too large.

Signed-off-by: Javier Tia <javier@peridio.com>
---
